---
import '../styles/content.css';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import DownloadCards from '../components/DownloadCards.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { backdrop } from '../lib/shots';
import { downloads, site } from '../data/site';

const art = await backdrop('console-game');

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Which file should I download?',
    a: 'On Windows, the installer. On Linux, the AppImage works on almost every distribution and updates itself; pick the .deb, .rpm or Arch package if you prefer your package manager.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does Windows show a SmartScreen warning?',
    a: 'The Windows files are not code-signed yet, so SmartScreen does not recognise them. Choose **More info**, then **Run anyway**. Every release is still signed with minisign, and Heronite checks that signature before it installs an update.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Heronite update itself?',
    a: 'It checks GitHub for a new release shortly after it starts and then every 6 hours. The AppImage, the Windows installer and the portable copy update in place; the .deb, .rpm and Arch packages install through `pkexec` after you confirm. See [Updates](/docs/installation/#updates).',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do I need on Linux?',
    a: 'A 64-bit (x86_64) system with a working Vulkan driver: Mesa for AMD and Intel, or the NVIDIA driver. Tools like gamescope, bubblewrap and Ludusavi are optional and each turns on a feature.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I run it from a USB drive?',
    a: 'Yes, with the Windows portable zip. The program, your Collection, settings and screenshots all stay in that folder. See [the portable copy](/docs/files-and-folders/#the-portable-copy-windows).',
  },
];

const softwareApplication = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'Heronite',
  url: `${site.url}/`,
  applicationCategory: 'GameApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Windows 10, Windows 11, Linux',
  downloadUrl: [downloads.windowsInstaller, downloads.windowsPortable, downloads.appImage],
  installUrl: `${site.url}/download/`,
  license: 'https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
};
---

<Base
  title="Download Heronite for Windows and Linux (Free)"
  description="Download Heronite, the free and open source game launcher: installer and portable zip for Windows 10 and 11, AppImage, .deb, .rpm and Arch packages for Linux."
  jsonLd={[softwareApplication]}
>
  <main id="main">
    <header class="index-hero scene" style={`--art:url(${art});--pos:60% 30%;--bright:.8`}>
      <div class="wrap">
        <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Download', href: '/download/' }]} />
        <p class="eyebrow">Download</p>
        <h1 class="h1">Download <em>Heronite</em></h1>
        <p class="lead">Free and open source under GPL-3.0. No account, no ads, no telemetry. For Windows 10 and 11 and any 64-bit Linux distribution.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap index-body">
      <DownloadCards level="h2" />

      <div class="article" style="margin-top:72px">
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <p>On this page</p>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#linux">Install on Linux</a></li>
            <li><a href="#windows">Install on Windows</a></li>
            <li><a href="#next">After installing</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="linux">Install on Linux</h2>
          <p>Every package is 64-bit (x86_64). Heronite draws its window with Vulkan, so you need a working Vulkan driver: Mesa for AMD and Intel, or the proprietary driver for NVIDIA.</p>
          <div class="table">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>File</th><th>Install</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Any distribution (<strong>recommended</strong>), SteamOS</td><td><code>Heronite-x86_64.AppImage</code></td><td><code>chmod +x Heronite-x86_64.AppImage && ./Heronite-x86_64.AppImage</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Debian 12+, Ubuntu 22.04+, Mint, Pop!_OS</td><td><code>heronite_&lt;version&gt;-1_amd64.deb</code></td><td><code>sudo apt install ./heronite_*_amd64.deb</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Fedora 36+, openSUSE</td><td><code>heronite-&lt;version&gt;-1.x86_64.rpm</code></td><td><code>sudo dnf install ./heronite-*.x86_64.rpm</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Arch, Manjaro, EndeavourOS, CachyOS</td><td><code>heronite-&lt;version&gt;-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst</code></td><td><code>sudo pacman -U heronite-*-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst</code></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Keep the AppImage in a folder you can write to, like <code>~/Applications</code>, so it can update itself. On the Steam Deck, follow the <a href="/guides/steam-deck/">Steam Deck guide</a>.</p>

          <h2 id="windows">Install on Windows</h2>
          <p>Windows 10 and 11 are supported. The <strong>installer</strong> installs Heronite for your user into <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Heronite</code>, with no administrator rights needed. The <strong>portable zip</strong> runs from any folder or USB drive and keeps your library, settings and screenshots in that folder.</p>
          <blockquote><p><strong>SmartScreen.</strong> The files are not code-signed yet, so Windows may say it protected your PC. Choose <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</p></blockquote>
          <p>Logging in to Epic Games, GOG or Amazon uses the Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime. Windows 11 has it; on Windows 10, <a href="https://developer.microsoft.com/microsoft-edge/webview2/">install it from Microsoft</a> if the login window asks for it.</p>

          <h2 id="next">After installing</h2>
          <p>On the first start Heronite finds your Steam games, your emulators and your ROM folders by itself. Then log in to your other stores on the <strong>Stores</strong> page. The <a href="/docs/getting-started/">getting started guide</a> walks through the rest, and the full <a href="/docs/installation/">installation docs</a> cover updates and removal.</p>
          <p>Prefer to build it yourself? The <a href={site.repo}>source code is on GitHub</a>, with every <a href={site.releases}>release</a> and its notes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top:56px">
        <Faq items={faq} title="Download questions" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
